among other things, anselm lingnau asks
	... is the TRIP test being upgraded to trigger the errors
	that surfaced `recently', ...?

i get a chance to look at dek's updates practically before anyone
else, and in my experience, for any bug fix that he feels can be
tested in trip, he adds some new code or changes some old.  note
that there are some changes made that he *doesn't* feel need trip
changes, or that are unsuitable for such a test.  this includes
such areas as memory management -- how much space is allocated to
a particular memory segment -- and other system-dependent features
or features that are explicitly permitted to be changed locally.

i believe it's probably wise, should a latex trip test be created,
to re-establish a "baseline" whenever a new version of tex is
released.  after all, some of the bug fixes may have been made as
a result of problems uncovered in latex jobs.
						-- bb


